// rustc nll/enum-drop-access.rs — upstream E0713
// imported batch B167 @ da5114692c9ebe46b869488c5f34f92eb10b98c1 (rust 1.100.0)
// ⚠ MOVED FROM THE ADMIT SHELF TO THE FAIL SHELF 2026-09-08, AND **NOT** FOR THE
//   DEFECT IT WAS ROWED FOR. The E0509 landing of that day refuses this program:
//   `fn f(x: DropStruct) -> &mut T { return x.field; }` moves a sub-value out of
//   a value that implements `Drop`, which rustc ALSO refuses as E0509, before
//   E0713 is ever reached. So the refusal is CORRECT and the program is
//   genuinely illegal — but the row's own root was the E0713 hole (a `&mut`
//   handed out PAST a destructor that then writes through it), and THAT HOLE IS
//   NOT CLOSED. It is now invisible here, because this program can no longer
//   reach it. Re-rowing it needs a program that gets the `&mut` out WITHOUT
//   moving a field out of a Drop value; filed in unrowed_backlog.ledger as
//   `e0713_mutref_past_destructor_needs_a_new_carrier`.
//   THIS WAS NOT PREDICTED. The prediction file for the E0509 half named 7 rows
//   and these two were not among them; they closed in the correct direction and
//   are reported as an unpredicted closing, not folded into the count.
// shelf: admit · root nllmoves.E
// ── BUCKET 4, IMPLEMENTATION HOLE — NOT RE-PORTABLE TODAY. 2026-09-07 (stage 5).
// Original path: tests/ui/nll/enum-drop-access.rs
// upstream commit: 4b0c9d76ae7d387229caea55cfa73c280b08b8a7
// upstream construct: lines 11-18 —
//     fn drop_enum(opt: DropOption<&mut i32>) -> Option<&mut i32> {
//         match opt {
//             DropOption::Some(&mut ref mut r) => { //~ ERROR
//                 Some(r)
//             },
//             DropOption::None => None,
//         }
//     }
//   THE NESTED PATTERN IS THE TEST. `&mut ref mut r` REBORROWS through the `&mut`
//   the enum payload holds, rather than moving it out, and upstream's point is that
//   `DropOption`'s destructor can still reach that payload while `r` is live. The
//   two OK cases in the same upstream file (`optional_tuple`, `different_variants`)
//   differ from it only in whether the dropped value can reach the reference — so a
//   port that flattens the pattern is testing a different question.
// Modifications: none — NOT PORTED AS-IS AND NOT PORTABLE AS-IS.
// MISSING FEATURE: nested patterns inside enum-variant payloads. MEASURED
//   2026-09-07 on the upstream shape, generic enum and `Drop` impl and all:
//     error [fn drop_enum]: pattern DropOption::Some: nested patterns inside
//       enum-variant payloads are not yet supported; bind to a name and match in
//       the body
//   The generic enum with a `Drop` impl compiles on its own; it is the nested
//   `&mut ref mut` inside `Some(..)` that has no path. Following the compiler's own
//   suggestion — bind a name and match in the body — SUBSTITUTES the construct, and
//   a re-port may not do that.
//   Backlog: `enum_payload_nested_pattern_absent` in tests/logos/unrowed_backlog.ledger.
// ⚠ WHAT IS BELOW IS THE OLD PORT, KEPT SO THE SHELF ENTRY STILL HAS A PROGRAM. It
//   is `fn drop_enum(opt: DropOpt) -> &mut i64 { return opt.r; }` — returning a
//   `&mut` field out of a by-value struct that implements `Drop`. That is a real
//   admission, but it is NOT this test: it is character for character the same
//   program as `issue-52059-report-when-borrow-and-drop-conflict`'s `finish_1`, and
//   the duplication is why the `nllmoves.E` grouping looked like one mechanism.
package enum_drop_access;
struct DropOpt { r: &mut i64 }
impl Drop for DropOpt { fn drop(self: &mut DropOpt) { *self.r = 9i64; } }
fn drop_enum(opt: DropOpt) -> &mut i64 { return opt.r; }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut n: i64 = 1i64;
    let d = DropOpt { r: &mut n };
    let out = drop_enum(d);
    *out = 5i64;
    return 0i32;
}
